1
00:00:03,090 --> 00:00:11,460
This lesson explains substructure reduction
in Mechanical within Ansys Workbench.

2
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:19,560
Many students find APDL difficult
to learn and get started with.

3
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:23,960
Working through a solution in Workbench

4
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:30,100
may help you build a solid
understanding of APDL.

5
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:35,580
However, APDL is relatively
easier to control.

6
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:43,000
For rail modeling and batch node creation,
APDL is more convenient than Workbench.

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,770
Workbench still uses APDL
commands to run the solution

8
00:00:52,290 --> 00:00:53,490
to reduce substructures.

9
00:00:54,720 --> 00:01:02,880
After learning Workbench, I recommend
studying the APDL solution workflow carefully.

10
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:11,120
This is a file I prepared earlier.

11
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:18,350
It was only a test, so we will recreate it.

12
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:25,960
We follow the commands in the
Simpack Help documentation.

13
00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:42,610
After completing the other
settings, we solve the substructure.

14
00:01:42,610 --> 00:01:44,510
The procedure is the same as before.

15
00:01:44,510 --> 00:01:51,910
These belong to a substructure
analysis step. The key is

16
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:58,620
to create a recovery
matrix. Let us take a look.

17
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:08,990
Select the appropriate retained-data
format for the recovery matrix.

18
00:02:09,550 --> 00:02:16,170
Here we retain the stiffness and
mass matrices. As for damping,

19
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:21,820
The imported [FBR/FBI: unclear]
has no damping in Simpack.

20
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:25,760
The damping must be assigned in Simpack.

21
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:28,380
Next,

22
00:02:29,610 --> 00:02:36,030
CMSOPT has little effect in this workflow.

23
00:02:36,270 --> 00:02:42,730
As shown here, it is
effective only in a Batch Job.

24
00:02:43,510 --> 00:02:48,770
You may include it for reference,
but it has no effect here.

25
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:55,680
CMSEL is used for our node selection,

26
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:00,180
selecting this component set.

27
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:04,880
Retained Node is the name
I created in Workbench.

28
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:11,060
For upper and lower node sets, use CMSEL.

29
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:17,580
After selecting the first set,
use A to add the other node set.

30
00:03:17,690 --> 00:03:21,210
For this beam-element model,
only one set is needed.

31
00:03:22,450 --> 00:03:27,420
Next is the M command.

32
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:32,590
This command includes
the entire selected set

33
00:03:32,590 --> 00:03:35,370
and retains all its degrees of freedom.

34
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:41,470
For OMEGA, no additional
force control is needed here.

35
00:03:41,470 --> 00:03:45,010
The solution setup is now complete.

36
00:03:45,010 --> 00:03:54,290
After solving the substructure in ANSYS,

37
00:03:54,290 --> 00:03:57,770
the output files are SUB and CDB.

38
00:03:57,770 --> 00:04:02,050
A CDB file is comparable
to an Abaqus INP file.

39
00:04:02,050 --> 00:04:05,490
The SUB file contains
the reduced superelement.

40
00:04:05,490 --> 00:04:10,410
TCMS is the file used to
represent the recovery matrix.

41
00:04:10,410 --> 00:04:12,910
A recovery matrix has already been created,

42
00:04:13,370 --> 00:04:20,650
but the results must be converted

43
00:04:20,650 --> 00:04:28,310
before they are written to a TCMS file.

44
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:32,130
In most cases, not converting
them causes no problem.

45
00:04:32,130 --> 00:04:37,990
Tests show that SUB and CDB
are generally sufficient.

46
00:04:38,630 --> 00:04:41,810
TCMS therefore has limited
practical value here.

47
00:04:41,810 --> 00:04:46,810
When importing it with
a compatibility version,

48
00:04:46,810 --> 00:04:52,070
or with Workbench, errors occur,
so I do not recommend using it.

49
00:04:52,810 --> 00:04:55,930
We also need a CDB file.

50
00:04:55,930 --> 00:05:01,670
Use the CDWRITE command

51
00:05:01,670 --> 00:05:06,830
to write the CDB file. The
IGES option can be omitted,

52
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:10,490
but including this
optional item is also fine.

53
00:05:10,490 --> 00:05:13,510
Now open Workbench.

54
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:18,620
Save this project

55
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:25,320
in this folder on the Desktop.

56
00:05:26,590 --> 00:05:30,770
Create a new project in Workbench.

57
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:33,890
Select New.

58
00:05:33,890 --> 00:05:41,020
I will provide this file later.

59
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:48,360
[Unclear phrase.]

60
00:05:54,930 --> 00:06:00,980
There is one more point I omitted earlier.

61
00:06:01,830 --> 00:06:08,760
CMSOPT has another option

62
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:15,790
that specifies CMS. As discussed earlier,

63
00:06:15,790 --> 00:06:18,510
it is a component mode synthesis method.

64
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:25,980
Among these methods, it is more
accurate than traditional substructuring

65
00:06:27,270 --> 00:06:30,070
in this context.

66
00:06:31,010 --> 00:06:38,750
Consult ANSYS material and
technical forums on CMS.

67
00:06:38,750 --> 00:06:43,390
For example, CMS uses
generalized coordinates,

68
00:06:43,390 --> 00:06:47,390
whereas traditional substructuring does not.

69
00:06:47,390 --> 00:06:53,430
It therefore describes the
dynamic behavior less completely.

70
00:06:53,430 --> 00:06:58,050
Under static loading, the
two methods differ little,

71
00:06:58,050 --> 00:07:01,830
but CMS is more accurate
for dynamic analysis.

72
00:07:01,830 --> 00:07:08,580
The original Substructure approach
is the traditional method.

73
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:11,120
Take a look here.

74
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:19,420
ANSYS uses this method, and
information is available online.

75
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:24,930
For dynamic simulation,
avoid the traditional method.

76
00:07:24,930 --> 00:07:28,390
Use the CMS component mode synthesis method.

77
00:07:28,950 --> 00:07:34,990
For this file, consult the Help website

78
00:07:34,990 --> 00:07:38,690
for the description of CMS.

79
00:07:38,690 --> 00:07:45,570
I will put the link in a
TXT file for reference.

80
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:47,780
Other websites also explain the method.

81
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:55,890
You can review comparisons
between the methods.

82
00:07:55,890 --> 00:08:02,410
For example, this article describes

83
00:08:02,410 --> 00:08:08,930
and compares several methods.

84
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:20,470
We perform a dynamic solution and enter

85
00:08:20,610 --> 00:08:28,110
the number of modes, such as 40.

86
00:08:28,110 --> 00:08:35,490
The first 6 ANSYS modes are
rigid-body modes. Excluding them

87
00:08:35,490 --> 00:08:42,790
leaves 40 minus 6, or 34 modes,
as the generated file will show.

88
00:08:42,790 --> 00:08:44,450
First, add a Modal analysis.

89
00:08:54,410 --> 00:08:57,950
First, define the material.

90
00:08:58,750 --> 00:09:02,650
We are using Structural Steel,
so no custom material is needed.

91
00:09:02,650 --> 00:09:06,250
For a different material,
create a new material here.

92
00:09:08,450 --> 00:09:18,400
Its principal properties include
elastic modulus and density.

93
00:09:19,350 --> 00:09:26,710
Enter those values if you
have custom material data.

94
00:09:26,710 --> 00:09:34,230
Under Isotropic Elasticity, also enter
the elastic modulus and Poisson's ratio.

95
00:09:34,230 --> 00:09:41,510
Our material is already defined,
so use Structural Steel directly.

96
00:09:41,970 --> 00:09:48,310
Next, import the geometry model.

97
00:09:49,270 --> 00:09:53,190
Previously, geometry was
imported with DesignModeler.

98
00:09:53,830 --> 00:09:59,110
I now find SpaceClaim more convenient
for direct geometry editing.

99
00:09:59,110 --> 00:10:07,000
Browse to the file and select New from
the SpaceClaim folder on the Desktop.

100
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,050
Click here.

101
00:10:10,790 --> 00:10:19,460
The file is loaded; click Refresh.

102
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:22,590
[?]

103
00:10:35,420 --> 00:10:36,620
This may be slow.

104
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:02,420
Open it here.

105
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:10,180
This...

106
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:29,880
may take a moment to open.

107
00:11:56,550 --> 00:12:02,210
Much of the Help documentation
can be downloaded

108
00:12:04,890 --> 00:12:12,190
as PDF files, including
descriptions of many modules.

109
00:12:12,190 --> 00:12:15,770
You can also search Help
directly for specific questions.

110
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:23,700
That is often more efficient than
searching the web for a tutorial.

111
00:12:23,700 --> 00:12:30,070
The geometry model is now imported.

112
00:12:30,070 --> 00:12:34,640
Press F7 to fit the
complete model in the view.

113
00:12:34,640 --> 00:12:39,270
With this display setting,

114
00:12:40,690 --> 00:12:43,430
inspect the geometry here.

115
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:50,320
The cross-section was loaded
directly at the beginning,

116
00:12:50,320 --> 00:12:55,120
so it does not need to be imported again.

117
00:12:55,120 --> 00:13:01,900
Its properties have already been calculated,
including cross-sectional area and moments of inertia.

118
00:13:01,900 --> 00:13:04,280
These values describe the
cross-section properties.

119
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:12,530
For the material, if a
different material is used,

120
00:13:12,530 --> 00:13:14,650
assign that material.

121
00:13:17,060 --> 00:13:21,960
The material is assigned here.

122
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:23,320
Select the body.

123
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:25,180
A custom material would appear here.

124
00:13:26,650 --> 00:13:31,610
Here, allow Structural Steel
to be assigned automatically.

125
00:13:31,610 --> 00:13:38,990
Only a line is displayed,
without its cross-section.

126
00:13:38,990 --> 00:13:45,370
Click the cross-section display option here,

127
00:13:49,290 --> 00:13:56,650
to show the complete profile.

128
00:13:56,650 --> 00:14:03,930
The beam line lies at the
cross-section centroid.

129
00:14:04,010 --> 00:14:10,660
Next, generate the mesh.

130
00:14:11,250 --> 00:14:16,830
Generate it once before
continuing. For a complex part,

131
00:14:18,110 --> 00:14:25,300
first confirm that the mesh can be
created correctly, then proceed with

132
00:14:25,300 --> 00:14:32,710
the remaining settings.

133
00:14:32,710 --> 00:14:39,770
To control the mesh more
precisely, insert a Sizing control.

134
00:14:41,510 --> 00:14:49,010
Select the geometry edges first,
then apply the Sizing control.

135
00:14:49,010 --> 00:14:53,850
The selection can be made
individually or with a box.

136
00:14:55,110 --> 00:15:01,990
Box selection failed earlier because
edges, not faces, must be selected.

137
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:09,500
Select these edges and apply the
control. The length here is 21 m.

138
00:15:09,500 --> 00:15:13,360
Typical sleeper spacing
is about 0.625 to 0.60 m,

139
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:20,600
so use 0.6 m. With solid elements,

140
00:15:20,600 --> 00:15:24,270
place one node at each rail fastener

141
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:31,340
and place another at midspan,
giving 0.3 m node spacing.

142
00:15:31,730 --> 00:15:35,730
For retained nodes, however, keep only

143
00:15:38,910 --> 00:15:45,630
the points spaced at 0.6 m. Let us check.

144
00:15:46,500 --> 00:15:51,490
This is the completed
mesh, with 0.6 m spacing.

145
00:15:53,470 --> 00:15:58,450
What is the most important next step?

146
00:15:59,070 --> 00:16:01,950
Orient the model along the Y direction.

147
00:16:03,460 --> 00:16:12,250
The interface is a little
slow. Align it with Y.

148
00:16:12,330 --> 00:16:15,370
This is now the longitudinal direction.

149
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:19,760
Next, select a node.

150
00:16:20,180 --> 00:16:23,200
There is an element-order setting here.

151
00:16:23,200 --> 00:16:29,420
With first order, or linear
elements, select this node.

152
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:38,610
Only the end nodes are selectable;
there is no midside node.

153
00:16:39,810 --> 00:16:45,750
If you select quadratic elements,

154
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:54,900
then make the same selection,

155
00:17:05,030 --> 00:17:08,290
a midside node appears in each element.

156
00:17:18,190 --> 00:17:25,000
That is the key difference. Here, use
Program Controlled for the mesh order

157
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,790
and regenerate the mesh.

158
00:17:44,740 --> 00:17:51,760
The previous choice was incorrect, but it
conveniently illustrates the difference.

159
00:17:52,030 --> 00:17:58,360
Select second order to inspect
the quadratic-element result.

160
00:18:09,470 --> 00:18:16,810
A node appears at each element
midpoint. With a linear

161
00:18:16,810 --> 00:18:23,400
element, there is no midside node.

162
00:18:28,210 --> 00:18:32,030
Be sure to switch to node selection, then

163
00:18:35,430 --> 00:18:42,930
return the mesh order to Program Controlled
so it is generated automatically.

164
00:18:42,930 --> 00:18:49,250
The next important task is selecting
the interface nodes to retain.

165
00:18:49,250 --> 00:18:56,650
Because the APDL command
will reference them,

166
00:18:56,650 --> 00:19:04,080
create a Named Selection here.

167
00:19:04,080 --> 00:19:13,750
The mesh spacing is 0.3 m,
so select every other node,

168
00:19:14,950 --> 00:19:22,750
This gives a better result.

169
00:19:23,380 --> 00:19:30,480
Box selection is convenient because
each required location must be selected.

170
00:19:30,620 --> 00:19:37,630
Hold Ctrl while selecting. This
is one node, and this is the next.

171
00:19:38,130 --> 00:19:45,260
This point should not be included,
so restart from the beginning.

172
00:19:49,490 --> 00:19:57,820
I do not recommend selecting this way when
there are many nodes, because it is inefficient.

173
00:20:11,120 --> 00:20:13,630
Select every other node
to obtain 0.6 m spacing.

174
00:20:26,550 --> 00:20:34,210
The operations look simple, but the
amount of repetitive work is substantial.

175
00:20:34,210 --> 00:20:38,340
After learning this method,

176
00:20:45,350 --> 00:20:47,730
you should still learn the APDL workflow.

177
00:20:47,730 --> 00:20:53,060
Use Retainnode [old text:
Retained; check speech].

178
00:20:53,060 --> 00:20:54,580
That is done.

179
00:21:01,950 --> 00:21:02,790
All right.

180
00:21:12,790 --> 00:21:14,730
the Named Selection has been created.

181
00:21:14,730 --> 00:21:21,840
Verify that it contains exactly these nodes.

182
00:21:22,950 --> 00:21:24,330
Next,

183
00:21:26,230 --> 00:21:33,530
run a modal analysis so that
the result can be inspected

184
00:21:33,530 --> 00:21:40,840
and verified. Configure
parallel processing here.

185
00:21:40,840 --> 00:21:45,510
Select the number of CPU cores; this
computer has four cores. Then solve.

186
00:21:53,680 --> 00:21:57,560
The solver output can be displayed here.

187
00:22:12,230 --> 00:22:18,250
A message confirms that these
commands were sent to the solver.

188
00:22:18,970 --> 00:22:26,450
No special action is
required; this is simply

189
00:22:26,450 --> 00:22:29,550
the output from the
complete solution process.

190
00:22:49,150 --> 00:22:56,320
Because the final objective
is substructure reduction,

191
00:22:57,450 --> 00:23:03,500
I originally omitted these result settings.
We will add them here for clarity.

192
00:23:03,920 --> 00:23:11,210
Leave the other items unchanged, then

193
00:23:11,210 --> 00:23:16,430
evaluate the results again.

194
00:23:17,980 --> 00:23:24,140
Solution time depends mainly on the mesh.
The mesh statistics show the node count.

195
00:23:24,140 --> 00:23:30,920
Tens of thousands of nodes are usually
acceptable, with reasonable solution speed.

196
00:23:30,920 --> 00:23:38,370
A much denser mesh
produces much larger files.

197
00:23:38,370 --> 00:23:45,500
The solver output
documents this information.

198
00:23:45,500 --> 00:23:49,680
The relevant details
should appear in this file.

199
00:23:49,680 --> 00:23:56,940
The first six are rigid-body modes
in the X, Y and Z directions;

200
00:23:56,940 --> 00:24:01,190
the later modes are the
structure's own modes.

201
00:24:01,190 --> 00:24:08,460
Now inspect the solution results.

202
00:24:08,460 --> 00:24:14,530
Beginning with mode 7,
display Total Deformation.

203
00:24:15,820 --> 00:24:22,880
Mode 7 moves in X. [Old text
also says Y: unconfirmed.]

204
00:24:28,210 --> 00:24:30,290
Inspect the animation.

205
00:24:37,400 --> 00:24:39,060
Then review the subsequent modes.

206
00:24:39,060 --> 00:24:44,550
No fixed constraints were applied,
so there are quite a few free modes.

207
00:24:44,550 --> 00:24:52,100
For example, inspect the
higher-frequency modes.

208
00:24:52,100 --> 00:25:01,110
It may be clearer to add a
constraint for this visualization.

209
00:25:01,310 --> 00:25:08,170
Otherwise, the calculated result... In Abaqus,
its effect on this structure is not very large.

210
00:25:10,160 --> 00:25:11,840
In other words, the free-free modes

211
00:25:18,670 --> 00:25:20,830
have little effect here.

212
00:25:23,050 --> 00:25:24,270
Stop this.

213
00:25:25,480 --> 00:25:33,470
Published comparisons
have examined this issue.

214
00:25:33,470 --> 00:25:35,990
It is not critical here.

215
00:25:35,990 --> 00:25:41,350
Evaluate the result.

216
00:25:41,350 --> 00:25:47,490
This mode

217
00:25:48,270 --> 00:25:54,990
This mode has a frequency
of 126.55 [unit not spoken].

218
00:25:54,990 --> 00:25:59,150
We can create result objects
for all of these modes.

219
00:26:02,960 --> 00:26:04,840
Delete this Total Deformation result.

220
00:26:05,590 --> 00:26:09,290
We only need to create

221
00:26:09,290 --> 00:26:17,120
the required modal results.

222
00:26:17,560 --> 00:26:20,730
Then export

223
00:26:20,730 --> 00:26:27,730
the natural modes as
well. Select all of them

224
00:26:27,730 --> 00:26:35,090
Then create the result shapes,
showing their deformation.

225
00:26:35,090 --> 00:26:42,570
Then evaluate all 40 results.

226
00:26:43,450 --> 00:26:50,910
Use Evaluate All Results
here, then inspect this mode.

227
00:26:50,910 --> 00:26:57,600
This result contains no rigid-body
motion; neither does the next mode.

228
00:27:02,060 --> 00:27:06,920
Save the file first, and
remember to save frequently.

229
00:27:24,320 --> 00:27:30,560
For a small model like this, use an
official licensed version or apply for

230
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:40,880
the student edition. The student
edition limits node and element counts,

231
00:27:42,330 --> 00:27:47,310
but it is sufficient for simple
models and is generally stable.

232
00:27:47,310 --> 00:27:47,970
This...

233
00:27:55,670 --> 00:28:02,690
starts a little slowly, but it is now open.

234
00:28:18,250 --> 00:28:23,980
Notice that the result objects
created earlier are gone.

235
00:28:26,190 --> 00:28:28,590
I will first

236
00:28:30,050 --> 00:28:31,390
Recreate it; this is quick.

237
00:28:39,820 --> 00:28:45,970
For example, align the generated
mesh in this direction.

238
00:28:50,790 --> 00:28:51,930
Run the solution briefly.

239
00:28:55,620 --> 00:28:57,180
Then select these nodes

240
00:28:57,180 --> 00:28:58,220
using box selection.

241
00:28:58,220 --> 00:29:02,840
This point must be selected.

242
00:29:18,300 --> 00:29:23,580
Although a Chinese version exists, I recommend
reading the English Help documentation.

243
00:29:24,530 --> 00:29:32,010
The English version is generally more
accurate and easier to interpret.

244
00:29:32,010 --> 00:29:38,890
Some Chinese technical translations are
poor, especially in the Fluent documentation.

245
00:29:40,630 --> 00:29:43,370
This section is OK
[Structural/DesignModeler?].

246
00:29:58,720 --> 00:30:05,880
Again, remember to save frequently.

247
00:30:05,880 --> 00:30:12,850
We selected the nodes
manually in this way, so

248
00:30:12,850 --> 00:30:19,570
a small oversight can cause problems.

249
00:30:24,760 --> 00:30:32,920
I strongly advise against this method: it
takes too much time and is inefficient.

250
00:30:41,730 --> 00:30:45,740
The underlying workflow still uses APDL.

251
00:30:46,630 --> 00:30:54,670
The most difficult parts of learning APDL are
often meshing, node handling, and node selection.

252
00:30:55,700 --> 00:31:01,240
With practice, these
tasks become manageable.

253
00:31:30,890 --> 00:31:33,630
Continue by creating a Named Selection.

254
00:31:43,430 --> 00:31:44,990
Rename it.

255
00:32:01,650 --> 00:32:02,270
Check it.

256
00:32:02,700 --> 00:32:03,960
This is correct.

257
00:32:03,960 --> 00:32:05,400
Then save it.

258
00:32:14,440 --> 00:32:15,200
Save it.

259
00:32:15,200 --> 00:32:17,480
Then run the solution again.

260
00:32:17,550 --> 00:32:18,710
Open Analysis Settings.

261
00:32:18,710 --> 00:32:21,730
For example, request 40 modes.

262
00:32:23,150 --> 00:32:25,730
Rail models can readily reach

263
00:32:26,930 --> 00:32:27,890
relatively high

264
00:32:32,390 --> 00:32:33,030
frequencies.

265
00:32:33,030 --> 00:32:37,420
About 30 to 40 modes are sufficient here.

266
00:32:38,870 --> 00:32:40,590
This is some of the solver output.

267
00:32:41,460 --> 00:32:42,060
In solving,

268
00:32:44,510 --> 00:32:46,210
Modal analysis is quick.

269
00:32:46,210 --> 00:32:49,150
The first six modes are
the rigid-body modes.

270
00:32:49,720 --> 00:32:51,660
Then inspect the solution results.

271
00:32:52,230 --> 00:32:56,110
Create deformation-shape
results for these modes.

272
00:32:57,630 --> 00:32:59,390
Create, then evaluate the results.

273
00:33:01,490 --> 00:33:02,830
After evaluation, save.

274
00:33:02,830 --> 00:33:05,990
The previous project was saved at this mode.

275
00:33:19,630 --> 00:33:20,730
A rigid-body mode.

276
00:33:20,730 --> 00:33:23,730
This is also a rigid-body mode.

277
00:33:25,930 --> 00:33:34,790
The following results show the first,
second, and third bending modes.

278
00:33:39,370 --> 00:33:44,370
These are the first through fourth modes in the
other direction; this is the fourth mode shape.

279
00:33:45,280 --> 00:33:47,500
Where are max and min displayed?

280
00:33:47,500 --> 00:33:50,710
They should be available
in the result object.

281
00:33:50,710 --> 00:33:55,020
Use a Probe to display
the maximum and minimum.

282
00:33:55,020 --> 00:34:01,490
You can also place the Probe at
any location to inspect its value.

283
00:34:05,330 --> 00:34:07,930
The modal solution is now complete.

284
00:34:07,930 --> 00:34:11,760
Next, insert a Commands object.

285
00:34:12,440 --> 00:34:19,210
It will contain the substructure-reduction
commands and related prompts.

286
00:34:19,700 --> 00:34:23,720
Enter the commands based on
the content explained earlier.

287
00:34:25,380 --> 00:34:30,540
These are general commands, similar
to CLEAN and CLEAR under Main Menu.

288
00:34:32,090 --> 00:34:35,250
They perform the same type of housekeeping.

289
00:34:35,360 --> 00:34:37,700
We will not discuss them here.

290
00:34:41,680 --> 00:34:44,810
Enter the fields here; prompts are provided.

291
00:34:47,360 --> 00:34:56,670
For example, set File Name to SUB.

292
00:35:00,100 --> 00:35:07,470
An invalid entry produces
a prompt. Then define

293
00:35:07,470 --> 00:35:14,690
the solution and set its analysis type.

294
00:35:15,050 --> 00:35:20,610
Use ANTYPE and select Substructure.

295
00:35:24,370 --> 00:35:33,790
Request stiffness and mass output:
the stiffness and mass matrices.

296
00:35:36,770 --> 00:35:42,250
Next, enable CMS.

297
00:35:42,940 --> 00:35:47,120
If the text is too small,
increase the font size.

298
00:35:47,120 --> 00:35:53,250
Adjust it here.

299
00:35:53,250 --> 00:36:01,620
Under CMSOPT, select component mode
synthesis and set the method to Free.

300
00:36:01,620 --> 00:36:07,520
Set the requested mode count to about 40.

301
00:36:09,130 --> 00:36:12,310
The remaining fields are optional.

302
00:36:16,700 --> 00:36:26,420
Then generate TCMS. As noted earlier, it is not necessary
here; the TCMS setting makes no difference here.

303
00:36:27,140 --> 00:36:33,870
Use CMSEL with option S
to select the component.

304
00:36:34,570 --> 00:36:37,410
Use Retainnode for Name
[check the spoken name].

305
00:36:38,770 --> 00:36:44,400
It must match the name on the
left, or the solution will fail.

306
00:36:45,900 --> 00:36:47,800
Set Type to NODE.

307
00:36:49,790 --> 00:36:56,310
Select all degrees of
freedom for these nodes.

308
00:36:56,310 --> 00:36:59,430
Select all the nodes for the component.

309
00:36:59,430 --> 00:37:08,250
If there are several components—for
example, upper and lower sets—use option A

310
00:37:08,250 --> 00:37:14,110
and create another Named Selection here

311
00:37:14,830 --> 00:37:15,610
to append it.

312
00:37:15,610 --> 00:37:19,270
Then use the M command
for all retained nodes.

313
00:37:19,730 --> 00:37:26,890
These are all the nodes to be retained,

314
00:37:26,890 --> 00:37:30,430
with every degree of freedom retained.

315
00:37:37,610 --> 00:37:39,030
Retain them accordingly.

316
00:37:47,770 --> 00:37:55,510
Save it. Keep the name consistent with
the earlier commands, then use CDWRITE

317
00:37:57,350 --> 00:38:00,750
to write the CDB file.

318
00:38:05,530 --> 00:38:11,620
Use file or sub [speech
unclear; screen: FILE,CDB].

319
00:38:14,480 --> 00:38:18,680
Here, use CDB consistently [speech unclear].

320
00:38:18,680 --> 00:38:25,790
Include the optional IGES argument as well.

321
00:38:25,790 --> 00:38:29,900
Run the solution.

322
00:38:29,900 --> 00:38:37,090
Add FINISH

323
00:38:37,090 --> 00:38:42,210
to terminate the command sequence.

324
00:38:44,500 --> 00:38:46,920
Now solve.

325
00:38:52,430 --> 00:38:54,550
Switch to the

326
00:38:55,870 --> 00:38:57,930
Solver Output window.

327
00:39:00,250 --> 00:39:02,810
This is an XML file viewed as a web page.

328
00:39:02,810 --> 00:39:05,990
It can be viewed in a
browser or by other means.

329
00:39:17,090 --> 00:39:18,990
Check whether any errors were reported.

330
00:39:22,110 --> 00:39:27,670
The calculation finished very quickly, but no
substructure-reduction output was produced.

331
00:39:27,840 --> 00:39:32,350
That indicates an error or warning.

332
00:39:32,350 --> 00:39:33,950
Inspect this section.

333
00:39:35,910 --> 00:39:38,930
Use Solver Output to
identify the likely cause.

334
00:39:39,970 --> 00:39:42,310
Open the corresponding folder.

335
00:39:44,130 --> 00:39:51,150
In this directory, inspect the error file.

336
00:39:58,010 --> 00:40:04,330
The path is dp0/SYS.

337
00:40:04,660 --> 00:40:09,760
Open the Mechanical subdirectory
and read the message.

338
00:40:13,930 --> 00:40:19,450
Data file FULL.db does not exist for RESUME.

339
00:40:24,810 --> 00:40:28,430
One of the commands must be incorrect.

340
00:40:30,580 --> 00:40:31,660
Check the commands.

341
00:40:34,300 --> 00:40:42,960
This entry should use FULL.

342
00:40:42,960 --> 00:40:43,580
So

343
00:40:54,180 --> 00:40:55,080
Paste it here.

344
00:40:55,080 --> 00:40:56,100
That is it.

345
00:40:56,950 --> 00:40:58,730
You can run it yourself.

346
00:40:58,730 --> 00:41:07,810
I must have mistyped one of the
entries during the previous run.

347
00:41:10,130 --> 00:41:11,530
The problem is here:

348
00:41:12,600 --> 00:41:13,900
the names do not match.

349
00:41:13,900 --> 00:41:22,180
I changed one occurrence to SUB but left
another as FULL, which caused the error.

350
00:41:22,410 --> 00:41:26,650
Solve again and inspect Solver Output.

351
00:41:37,350 --> 00:41:41,870
The eigenfrequency solution is now running.

352
00:41:51,900 --> 00:41:59,810
Inspect the result; an
error message appears.

353
00:42:02,570 --> 00:42:10,010
The speaker says this error does not affect
the result. Then inspect the directory.

354
00:42:10,010 --> 00:42:16,630
Inspect this directory.

355
00:42:19,580 --> 00:42:21,900
Solver Output shows that the run completed.

356
00:42:21,900 --> 00:42:22,920
We can continue.

357
00:42:22,920 --> 00:42:32,370
Because the frequencies have already been calculated,
the stiffness and mass matrices are generated.

358
00:42:32,370 --> 00:42:35,890
The warning can therefore be ignored.

359
00:42:35,890 --> 00:42:37,950
Workbench Mechanical gives this warning.

360
00:42:38,510 --> 00:42:46,950
The output window may also report that
some content exceeds its display range.

361
00:42:46,950 --> 00:42:51,350
A finer mesh produces more output and
can exceed the window's display limit.

362
00:42:51,750 --> 00:43:00,970
Right-click here and select
Open Solver Files Directory.

363
00:43:03,190 --> 00:43:07,370
After opening the
directory, take the OUT file

364
00:43:08,230 --> 00:43:14,290
and open it in a text editor.

365
00:43:15,710 --> 00:43:20,250
The complete solver output
can be reviewed there.

366
00:43:20,930 --> 00:43:26,400
The completed and commented
lines shown here can be ignored.

367
00:43:28,490 --> 00:43:36,010
This completes the
file-generation procedure.

368
00:43:36,950 --> 00:43:41,630
I still recommend

369
00:43:44,040 --> 00:43:48,570
the more explicit

370
00:43:49,850 --> 00:43:52,370
APDL workflow.

371
00:43:52,930 --> 00:44:00,830
This Workbench procedure performs only substructure
reduction and exposes only part of the results.

372
00:44:01,750 --> 00:44:05,010
The current error prevents the mode
shapes from being displayed here.

373
00:44:05,950 --> 00:44:12,130
If they cannot be displayed, connect the
model data to another analysis system.

374
00:44:14,310 --> 00:44:20,700
Share the data here, then

375
00:44:22,060 --> 00:44:28,430
remove the mode-order setting
from this result branch

376
00:44:29,030 --> 00:44:35,710
and create a shared

377
00:44:35,710 --> 00:44:41,650
Modal system to display the mode shapes.

378
00:44:41,650 --> 00:44:49,870
This makes both validation and
visualization easier to understand.

379
00:45:12,630 --> 00:45:17,310
Delete these deformation result objects.

380
00:45:22,830 --> 00:45:25,690
This does not affect the
substructure solution settings.

381
00:45:30,500 --> 00:45:38,970
The number of modes has
already been set in CMS.

382
00:45:43,040 --> 00:45:43,390
modes.

383
00:45:49,250 --> 00:45:53,650
For example, increase the mode
count under Analysis Settings

384
00:45:53,650 --> 00:45:58,370
to 40.

385
00:45:59,890 --> 00:46:03,860
Then solve.

386
00:46:03,860 --> 00:46:04,280
Now

387
00:46:24,840 --> 00:46:32,480
create the display results

388
00:46:33,410 --> 00:46:35,030
and evaluate them.

389
00:46:35,030 --> 00:46:41,190
Wait for the model to finish.

390
00:46:55,220 --> 00:46:56,360
Let the solution run.

391
00:46:57,230 --> 00:46:58,110
Neither affects it.

392
00:47:05,040 --> 00:47:06,240
Inspect the mode shapes.

393
00:47:06,240 --> 00:47:13,860
The initial modes are rigid-body
modes. These are modes 1 and 2.

394
00:47:15,390 --> 00:47:17,890
The mesh is too coarse
for a clear visualization.

395
00:47:17,890 --> 00:47:18,970
Make it somewhat finer.

396
00:47:18,970 --> 00:47:20,650
The overall display will be clearer.

397
00:47:21,970 --> 00:47:25,110
This looks better.

398
00:47:31,120 --> 00:47:36,470
For example, set the size
to 0.01 [unit not spoken],

399
00:47:43,590 --> 00:47:45,940
and regenerate it.

400
00:48:01,220 --> 00:48:05,490
Then evaluate the results again.

401
00:48:19,590 --> 00:48:25,170
Solve again. Remeshing changes the results.

402
00:48:47,890 --> 00:48:52,370
Be sure to save first.

403
00:48:56,860 --> 00:48:57,440
It is slow.

404
00:48:59,740 --> 00:49:05,770
A finer mesh is recommended; the
speaker says it is more efficient.

405
00:49:09,370 --> 00:49:11,230
Relatively speaking, it is higher.

406
00:49:13,770 --> 00:49:16,950
We will not recalculate this model now.

407
00:49:42,850 --> 00:49:50,170
The fine mesh makes the deformation
shape look more detailed.

408
00:49:50,170 --> 00:49:54,350
You can also hide the mesh.

409
00:50:00,410 --> 00:50:09,810
Either display mode is acceptable for this

410
00:50:16,250 --> 00:50:17,910
result.

411
00:50:17,910 --> 00:50:25,400
Where is the mesh display option?

412
00:50:25,400 --> 00:50:32,880
Turn off the mesh display

413
00:50:32,880 --> 00:50:41,670
and select the smooth contour view.

414
00:50:41,670 --> 00:50:48,430
This produces a smooth contour plot.

415
00:50:53,790 --> 00:51:01,350
Set the edge display to No
Wireframe and inspect the result.

416
00:51:01,350 --> 00:51:08,530
This is the first mode.

417
00:51:08,530 --> 00:51:09,830
Now the second mode:

418
00:51:16,850 --> 00:51:17,730
inspect its shape.

419
00:51:52,530 --> 00:52:00,360
Rendering is slow, but these
animations show the mode shapes.

420
00:52:01,070 --> 00:52:06,750
By modes 30 to 40, the frequencies
are already quite high.

421
00:52:08,850 --> 00:52:12,050
Adjust this setting, for example.

422
00:52:12,440 --> 00:52:13,620
Wait for it to display.

423
00:52:17,590 --> 00:52:22,310
This is one complete mode shape.

424
00:52:22,310 --> 00:52:31,040
These results can then be exported.

425
00:52:31,040 --> 00:52:33,980
This section also demonstrated
several Workbench operations.

426
00:52:34,400 --> 00:52:37,440
Workbench is relatively concise

427
00:52:38,970 --> 00:52:42,810
and the overall workflow
is easier to understand.

428
00:52:43,520 --> 00:52:50,480
Nevertheless, I recommend learning APDL because
it improves workflow control, efficiency,

429
00:52:50,480 --> 00:52:56,460
and understanding of the complete
substructure-reduction process.

430
00:52:57,340 --> 00:53:01,300
This concludes today's lesson.

431
00:53:01,300 --> 00:53:04,200
There is one additional
point I omitted earlier.

432
00:53:04,200 --> 00:53:09,450
After saving the project, archive it.

433
00:53:10,480 --> 00:53:12,960
I will add that step here.

434
00:53:14,170 --> 00:53:17,610
Select Archive.

435
00:53:20,020 --> 00:53:25,260
Workbench packages the
project into a WBPZ file.

436
00:53:25,260 --> 00:53:28,880
You can then move the project
by copying this single archive.

437
00:53:29,830 --> 00:53:32,150
Add finish/finished as
a suffix [unconfirmed].

438
00:53:32,150 --> 00:53:32,810
All right.

439
00:53:40,660 --> 00:53:41,920
Include the results.

440
00:53:42,420 --> 00:53:50,620
A finer mesh produces larger solver files.

441
00:53:50,620 --> 00:53:58,890
The mesh value [expression unclear];
the result files are about 36 GB.

442
00:53:58,890 --> 00:54:03,530
Consider how mesh density
affects results and file size.

443
00:54:07,020 --> 00:54:09,960
This is the packaged archive.

444
00:54:09,960 --> 00:54:11,160
Only just over 20 MB.

445
00:54:12,490 --> 00:54:15,090
This concludes the lesson.

446
00:54:15,090 --> 00:54:21,790
Next, we will generate an [FDR/FBI:
unclear] elastic-body file in Simpack.
